            "text": "ROOT CAUSE:\nIn Java, the `==` operator checks whether two `String` variables point to the exact same memory location rather than comparing their text contents.\n\nBEGINNER EXPLANATION:\nWhen working with objects like `String` in Java, using `==` does not check if the words are the same; it checks if they are the exact same object in memory. Because `sc.nextLine()` creates a new `String` object, `answer == \"yes\"` evaluates to `false`. To compare the actual text inside two strings, use the `.equals()` method instead (for example, `answer.equals(\"yes\")`).\n\nPATCHED CODE:\n```java\nimport java.util.Scanner;\npublic class J10 {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);\n        String answer = sc.nextLine();\n        if (answer.equals(\"yes\")) {\n            System.out.println(\"accepted\");\n        } else {\n            System.out.println(\"rejected\");\n        }\n    }\n}\n```",
